1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,359
A volunteer spends an hour mentoring a

2
00:00:02,359 --> 00:00:02,732
child.

3
00:00:02,732 --> 00:00:05,215
Before she leaves, she learns the family

4
00:00:05,215 --> 00:00:07,884
needs diapers tonight, so she posts a
brief

5
00:00:07,884 --> 00:00:08,319
update.

6
00:00:08,319 --> 00:00:10,740
By morning it is buried under dozens of

7
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:11,299
messages.

8
00:00:11,299 --> 00:00:13,162
The hour may never be counted.

9
00:00:13,162 --> 00:00:15,148
The family may still be waiting.

10
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,675
This is the problem behind challenge five:

11
00:00:20,675 --> 00:00:23,414
coordinators rebuild their week from
chats,

12
00:00:23,414 --> 00:00:26,217
texts and photos — forty volunteers,
roughly

13
00:00:26,217 --> 00:00:28,828
four hours a week, reconstructed by hand.

14
00:00:28,828 --> 00:00:31,376
Volunteer Relay turns those updates into

15
00:00:31,376 --> 00:00:34,051
traceable records, and needs someone owns.

16
00:00:34,051 --> 00:00:36,853
Here is the coordinator's inbox, with
review

17
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:37,681
work waiting.

18
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:40,533
Volunteers report in their own words.

19
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:42,887
This update describes one shared garden

20
00:00:42,887 --> 00:00:45,483
visit, two volunteers, and the time each
of

21
00:00:45,483 --> 00:00:46,087
them gave.

22
00:00:46,087 --> 00:00:48,139
Relay proposes an activity and two

23
00:00:48,139 --> 00:00:50,493
contributions, and keeps every proposed

24
00:00:50,493 --> 00:00:52,606
field tied to the original message.

25
00:00:52,606 --> 00:00:54,960
Nothing is approved until staff decide.

26
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,721
One message holds a visit for two people.

27
00:01:02,721 --> 00:01:05,023
One shared hour means one visit and two

28
00:01:05,023 --> 00:01:07,621
volunteer-hours, so approving it records
two

29
00:01:07,621 --> 00:01:09,924
separate contributions of sixty minutes

30
00:01:09,924 --> 00:01:10,219
each.

31
00:01:10,219 --> 00:01:12,640
Staff can inspect the evidence first, and

32
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,237
anything missing or unresolved stays
visible

33
00:01:15,237 --> 00:01:16,713
instead of being guessed.

34
00:01:16,713 --> 00:01:17,835
Nothing is guessed.

35
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:24,631
A later update describes the same visit.

36
00:01:24,631 --> 00:01:26,962
Counting every message would inflate the

37
00:01:26,962 --> 00:01:29,060
totals, so Relay raises the possible

38
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:30,634
duplicate for staff review.

39
00:01:30,634 --> 00:01:32,907
Rejecting it leaves the approved ledger

40
00:01:32,907 --> 00:01:35,355
unchanged, and the decision stays attached

41
00:01:35,355 --> 00:01:36,171
to its source.

42
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:44,942
An urgent need should never wait on a
buried

43
00:01:44,942 --> 00:01:45,422
message.

44
00:01:45,422 --> 00:01:48,065
Staff assign an owner, set an
acknowledgment

45
00:01:48,065 --> 00:01:50,406
deadline, and later record the outcome.

46
00:01:50,406 --> 00:01:52,568
If acknowledgment stalls, the worker

47
00:01:52,568 --> 00:01:54,129
escalates at the deadline.

48
00:01:54,129 --> 00:01:56,471
Delivery is not acknowledgment, and the

49
00:01:56,471 --> 00:01:58,813
board shows what still needs attention.

50
00:01:58,813 --> 00:02:00,975
An acknowledgment is a person taking

51
00:02:00,975 --> 00:02:03,317
responsibility, not a provider receipt.

52
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,813
At the end of the week the report comes
from

53
00:02:06,813 --> 00:02:09,097
the same records the team used to do its

54
00:02:09,097 --> 00:02:09,383
work.

55
00:02:09,383 --> 00:02:11,724
Approved hours, contributions, open needs

56
00:02:11,724 --> 00:02:14,180
and pending items are counted
consistently,

57
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,578
and unknown time stays unknown rather than

58
00:02:16,578 --> 00:02:17,378
becoming zero.

59
00:02:17,378 --> 00:02:19,776
Every decision is kept in an audit history

60
00:02:19,776 --> 00:02:22,232
with its source, so the report and the
work

61
00:02:22,232 --> 00:02:23,717
share one source of truth.

62
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:27,309
Volunteer Relay.

63
00:02:27,309 --> 00:02:29,896
Every hour counted, every need connected.

64
00:02:29,896 --> 00:02:32,483
Built for nonprofit teams, with fictional

65
00:02:32,483 --> 00:02:35,259
demo data and an open-source
implementation.

66
00:02:35,259 --> 00:02:37,972
The repository holds the code, the plan
and

67
00:02:37,972 --> 00:02:40,685
the recording notes, including the
fixtures

68
00:02:40,685 --> 00:02:41,316
used here.
